#!/usr/bin/env python3

"""tvcal-backend - calendar and to-do helper for the tvcal Turbo Vision client.

Everything about the data lives here (iCalendar files, repeats, reminders, the
to-do list, what other programs ask of tvcal); the C++ front end only sees a few
small TAB-separated replies.  tvcal runs it as a co-process, like tvmail and
tvchat run theirs.  Stdlib only, Python >= 3.6.

The data is one standard iCalendar file (RFC 5545), VEVENTs and VTODOs, so any
other calendar program can read it and `import` / `export` / `mail-event` speak
the same format:  ~/.local/share/tvcal/calendar.ics  ($TVCAL_DATA, or [cal] file
in the configuration).  Times are the machine's local time ("floating"); an
imported time with a Z is converted to local, a TZID is taken as local.

With a [server] url in the configuration the calendar lives on a CalDAV server (Radicale
on the mail/chat host, see configure/configure-radicale.sh): every verb reads it there (a
copy in ~/.local/state/tvcal/cache.ics serves reads for 15 s and while the server is down)
and a changed item is PUT back.  Without one, the local file is the calendar.

Configuration (optional - tvcal needs none): ~/.config/tvcal/tvcal.conf
($TVCAL_CONF).  See configure/tvcal.conf.example.

Replies are TAB-separated lines; a TAB, newline, carriage return or backslash
inside a field is written \\t \\n \\r \\\\.  Status 0 = ok, 1 = a message for the
user in the body, 2 = trouble.  Dates are YYYY-MM-DD, times HH:MM.

  ping                      'pong'
  range FROM TO             every occurrence of an event from FROM to TO (dates,
                            inclusive), repeats expanded, one line each:
                            date, start, end, uid, summary, location, repeat, remind
                            (start/end empty for an all-day event; an event of
                            several days appears on each of them)
  show UID                  event: 'event', uid, summary, location, date, start, end,
                            days, repeat, interval, until, remind, notes
                            to-do: 'todo', uid, summary, due, prio, status, cats, notes
  add-event [options] SUMMARY       prints the new uid
  set-event UID [options]           options: --date --start --end --days --location
                            --notes --repeat none|daily|weekly|monthly|yearly
                            --interval N --until DATE --remind MINUTES|off
                            (an empty value clears; dates take today, tomorrow,
                            +N, a weekday name, MM-DD, YYYY-MM-DD)
  todos [--all]             open to-dos (all with --all), ordered by due date then
                            priority: uid, status, prio, due, summary, cats
  add-todo [--due D] [--prio 0-9] [--cats C] [--notes N] SUMMARY
  set-todo UID [same options] [--summary S]
  done UID [--undo]         tick a to-do off (or back on)
  rm UID                    delete an event or a to-do
  find TEXT                 events (a year either side of today) and to-dos that
                            mention TEXT
  due                       reminders that fell due since the last call (each is
                            told once): 'event', uid, date, start, summary, minutes
                            to go; 'todo', uid, due, summary, 'overdue'|'today'
  describe UID [DATE]       one line about it, for a chat or a mail
  ics [UID]                 iCalendar text of one item, or of everything
  import FILE               add the events and to-dos in an .ics file
  floyd TEXT                Floyd's answer: !todo / !cal / !event on this calendar, !calc, !convert, !time ...
  settings                  week_start, remind, file, mode (local | remote)
  mail-event UID ADDRESS    mail an invitation (an .ics part) through tvmail
  has-tvmail                'yes' or 'no'
  requests                  take in what other programs left in the requests
                            directory; prints 'notice' lines
  pending                   what is waiting for a yes: id, kind, source, summary, date
  pending-get ID            the fields, as 'show' (kind 'event' or 'todo')
  pending-drop ID
  check-config FILE         errors and warnings for a tvcal.conf
  serve                     persistent mode: one shell-quoted request per line on
                            stdin, one framed reply ("STATUS NBYTES\\n" + bytes);
                            a "0 5\\nready" banner first

Requests (other programs write a one-line file NAME.req into
$TVCAL_REQUESTS, else ~/.local/state/tvcal/requests):
  todo TEXT            a to-do
  event TEXT           an event: [YYYY-MM-DD] [HH:MM[-HH:MM]] title
  eml PATH             a mail message (its calendar part, else its subject)
  ics PATH             an .ics file
  kind@source (todo@tvmail ...) says who asked.  Nothing is added until the
  user says yes in tvcal (Calendar > Incoming...).
"""
import sys, os, io, re, argparse, configparser, datetime as dt, json, shlex, time, uuid
import calendar as _cal
import email, email.policy, email.message, email.utils, shutil, subprocess, logging

